"An identity federation diagram shows which systems accept identity assertions from which providers and under what trust configuration.\n\nUse when: Review cross-domain sign-in and delegated identity trust. Explain claim mapping and relying-party relationships.\n\nAvoid when: Exact authorization-code message order is the principal question.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes identify identity providers, brokers, relying parties, and protected applications. Directed trusts state which party accepts assertions from another. Annotations name protocol, audience, issuer, and relevant claim transformations.\n\nBuild: Define the identity domains and the desired sign-in path. Identify each assertion issuer and relying party. Add trust configuration, claim mapping, and account-linking boundaries. Group domains in padded regions and route federation links around domain titles.\n\nCheck: Trust direction means assertion acceptance rather than user navigation. Authentication trust is distinguished from resource authorization. Issuer, audience, and account-linking assumptions are explicit. Claim annotations and cross-domain arrows do not obscure node text.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming federation automatically grants application permissions. Treating matching email strings as sufficient proof that identities are the same.\n\nExample: A corporate identity provider issues assertions accepted by a cloud relying party, which maps approved claims into a scoped role.\n\nAlternatives: OAuth authorization diagram, Authorization graph."
